DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Shakeout · 5 km
DESCRIPTION:• Very easy\, 5:45/km or slower\, plus 3 strides. Lay out you
 r kit\, pin your bib\, count your gels\, and plan the trip to Devonport.\n
 \nWhy: Settles the nerves and loosens the legs.\n\nFuel: Keep carb loading
 : your carb-load amount (see the Fuel tab). Have an early dinner you've ea
 ten before.\n\nHeart rate guide (estimated from your recent runs\, may be 
 off):\n• 5:45–6:30/km: easy\, HR 125–150. Over 150 means slow down\n
 \nRUN GUIDE\n\nHow it should feel: Very easy and short\, just shaking the 
 legs out.\n\nHow to run it:\n• 5:45/km or slower\n• 3 × 20 s relaxed 
 strides near the end\n\nElevation: Flat. Near Devonport is ideal if you're
  staying close to the start.\n\nBack off if:\n• Anything tight: skip the
  strides\n\nAfterwards: Lay out your kit\, pin your bib\, count your gels\
 , and plan the trip to Devonport. Early dinner you've eaten before.\n\nhtt
 ps://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
URL:https://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:akl-2026-11-01@joshuamorley.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261028T215149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Auckland Marathon · 42.2 km
DESCRIPTION:• Target 2:59:20 at 4:15/km. See the Race day tab for splits 
 and checks.\n\nFuel: Breakfast 3 h before\, gel 1 10–15 min before the s
 tart\, then your race gel schedule.\n\nHeart rate guide (estimated from yo
 ur recent runs\, may be off):\n• 4:12–4:18/km: HR about 158–170\n\nR
 UN GUIDE\n\nHow it should feel: The first 10 km should feel too easy. The 
 race starts at 32 km.\n\nHow to run it:\n• Target 2:59:20 at 4:15/km\n
 • Halfway about 25 s behind even pace\, because the hills and the bridge
  come first\n• Bridge: keep the effort level\, let it cost 10–20 s/km\
 n• After 32 km: hold form\, take your last gels\, and race the people in
  front\n\nHeart rate:\n• About 158–170 through halfway. Over 175 at ha
 lfway: ease to 4:25\n• HR drifts up late\, so judge by effort after 32 k
 m\n\nElevation: About 217 m of climbing\, though estimates vary (the cours
 e GPX gives anywhere from about 110 to 345 m depending on smoothing\, and 
 it has no bridge height). The hardest of it is in the first half: the Nort
 h Shore hills\, then the bridge from about km 15 to 17. The second half\, 
 out along Tamaki Drive and back\, is mostly flat with a few small bumps.\n
 \nBack off if:\n• See the checks on the Race day tab\n\nAfterwards: Walk
 \, eat and drink. Then celebrate.\n\nhttps://joshuamorley.github.io/runnin
 g-plan/#plan
URL:https://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:akl-2026-11-05@joshuamorley.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261028T215149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261105
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261106
SUMMARY:Recovery jog · 4 km
DESCRIPTION:• 5:45–6:15/km\, slower is fine\n• Stop and walk home if 
 anything feels sore rather than stiff\n\nWhy: Gets blood moving through ti
 red legs without adding damage.\n\nFuel: None needed. Eat a normal carb-ba
 sed meal afterwards.\n\nHeart rate guide (estimated from your recent runs\
 , may be off):\n• 5:45–6:15/km: easy\, HR 125–150. Over 150 means sl
 ow down\n\nRUN GUIDE\n\nHow it should feel: Shuffly and slow. Stiff is nor
 mal. Sharp or one-sided pain is not.\n\nHow to run it:\n• 5:45–6:15/km
 \, or slower if that's what your legs give you\n• Walking breaks are fin
 e\n\nElevation: Flat. Downhills load the quads that the marathon damaged m
 ost.\n\nBack off if:\n• Sore rather than stiff\, or limping: walk home a
 nd try again Saturday\n\nAfterwards: Nothing special. Keep eating well. Mu
 scle repair needs protein.\n\nhttps://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/
 #plan
URL:https://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:akl-2026-11-07@joshuamorley.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261028T215149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261107
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261108
SUMMARY:Easy run · 5 km
DESCRIPTION:• 5:30–6:00/km\, HR under 145\n• A parkrun jogged easy co
 unts. Don't race it\n\nWhy: Checks how the legs are coming back.\n\nFuel: 
 None needed. Eat a normal carb-based meal afterwards.\n\nHeart rate guide 
 (estimated from your recent runs\, may be off):\n• 5:30–6:00/km: easy\
 , HR 125–150. Over 150 means slow down\n\nRUN GUIDE\n\nHow it should fee
 l: Easy and relaxed. Probably still a bit flat. That's normal a week out f
 rom a marathon.\n\nHow to run it:\n• 5:30–6:00/km from the first step\
 n• A parkrun is fine if you jog it. Don't get pulled into racing\n\nElev
 ation: Flat. Under 20 m of climbing\, like the Good George course (+5 m) o
 r your usual morning loop.\n\nBack off if:\n• HR over 145 at easy pace: 
 slow down. Your body is still recovering\n\nAfterwards: Normal meals.\n\nh
 ttps://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
URL:https://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:akl-2026-11-10@joshuamorley.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261028T215149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Tuesday 5k · easy · 5 km
DESCRIPTION:• 5:00–5:15/km. Let the racers go. Too soon after a maratho
 n to race.\n\nWhy: Back into the Tuesday routine without the effort.\n\nFu
 el: None needed. Eat a normal carb-based meal afterwards.\n\nHeart rate gu
 ide (estimated from your recent runs\, may be off):\n• 5:00–5:15/km: e
 asy\, HR 125–150. Over 150 means slow down\n\nRUN GUIDE\n\nHow it should
  feel: Easy. You may feel ready to race. It's too early\, so let them go.\
 n\nHow to run it:\n• 5:00–5:15/km the whole way\n\nElevation: Flat. Un
 der 20 m of climbing\, like the Good George course (+5 m) or your usual mo
 rning loop.\n\nBack off if:\n• Anything sore: drop to 5:30 or jog it\n\n
 Afterwards: Normal dinner.\n\nhttps://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/
 #plan
URL:https://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:akl-2026-11-12@joshuamorley.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261028T215149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261112
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261113
SUMMARY:Easy run · readiness check · 6 km
DESCRIPTION:• 5:20–5:45/km\n• The check: HR under 150 at 5:20–5:30/
 km\, and stairs and hills feel normal\n• Both yes: race Kerikeri. Either
  no: run it steady. See the Kerikeri tab\n\nWhy: The simplest honest test 
 of whether the marathon has worn off.\n\nFuel: None needed. Eat a normal c
 arb-based meal afterwards.\n\nHeart rate guide (estimated from your recent
  runs\, may be off):\n• 5:20–5:45/km: easy\, HR 125–150. Over 150 me
 ans slow down\n\nRUN GUIDE\n\nHow it should feel: Easy and normal. This ru
 n is the check for whether you race Kerikeri.\n\nHow to run it:\n• 5:20
 –5:45/km\n• Note your average HR over km 2–5 at 5:20–5:30/km\n• 
 Go up a flight of stairs afterwards and notice whether your quads complain
 \n\nHeart rate:\n• Under 150 at 5:20–5:30\, as before Auckland: recove
 red\n• Over 150\, or it feels like work: not yet. Run Kerikeri steady\n\
 nElevation: Flat. Under 20 m of climbing\, like the Good George course (+5
  m) or your usual morning loop.\n\nBack off if:\n• Anything sharp: stop.
  Kerikeri is optional\, the next season isn't\n\nAfterwards: Pick your Ker
 ikeri target on the Kerikeri tab.\n\nhttps://joshuamorley.github.io/runnin
 g-plan/#plan
URL:https://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:akl-2026-11-14@joshuamorley.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261028T215149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261114
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261115
SUMMARY:Easy run + strides · 8 km
DESCRIPTION:• 5:20–5:40/km\, then 4 × 20 s relaxed strides.\n\nWhy: Th
 e strides remind your legs how to run fast again.\n\nFuel: None needed. Ea
 t a normal carb-based meal afterwards.\n\nHeart rate guide (estimated from
  your recent runs\, may be off):\n• 5:20–5:40/km: easy\, HR 125–150.
  Over 150 means slow down\n\nRUN GUIDE\n\nHow it should feel: Easy\, with 
 the strides feeling quick and light. Your legs should feel like yours agai
 n.\n\nHow to run it:\n• 7 km at 5:20–5:40/km\n• 4 × 20 s strides: b
 uild to about 3:30/km pace\, stay tall and relaxed\n• 60 s walk or jog b
 etween strides\n\nElevation: Flat. Under 20 m of climbing\, like the Good 
 George course (+5 m) or your usual morning loop.\n\nBack off if:\n• Anyt
 hing tight: skip the strides\n\nAfterwards: Normal meals.\n\nhttps://joshu
 amorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
URL:https://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:akl-2026-11-15@joshuamorley.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261028T215149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261115
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261116
SUMMARY:Easy long-ish run · 10 km
DESCRIPTION:• 5:20–5:40/km\, HR under 150\n• Include some gentle down
 hill if you can. Kerikeri drops about 275 m\n\nWhy: Your longest run since
  Auckland. The downhill gets your quads ready for the course.\n\nFuel: Non
 e needed. Eat a normal carb-based meal afterwards.\n\nHeart rate guide (es
 timated from your recent runs\, may be off):\n• 5:20–5:40/km: easy\, H
 R 125–150. Over 150 means slow down\n\nRUN GUIDE\n\nHow it should feel: 
 Easy and comfortable. Full sentences the whole way.\n\nHow to run it:\n•
  5:20–5:40/km\n• If your route has a gentle downhill\, run it at the s
 ame easy effort with quick\, light steps\n\nElevation: Some gentle downhil
 l is useful: 50–100 m of descent gets your quads used to it before Kerik
 eri. Avoid anything steep.\n\nBack off if:\n• HR over 150: slow down\n
 • Sore quads on the downhill: run the rest flat\n\nAfterwards: Carbs and
  protein within an hour.\n\nhttps://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#p
 lan
URL:https://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:akl-2026-11-17@joshuamorley.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261028T215149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261117
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261118
SUMMARY:Tuesday 5k · half-marathon pace · 5 km
DESCRIPTION:• Run it at your Kerikeri goal pace\, about 4:00–4:05/km fo
 r 1:25. Not a race\n• Race shoes\, to check they still feel right\n\nWhy
 : Your one session at goal pace\, four days out. Enough to feel sharp with
 out tiring you.\n\nFuel: None during. Have carbs and protein within an hou
 r afterwards.\n\nHeart rate guide (estimated from your recent runs\, may b
 e off):\n• 4:00–4:05/km: HR about 165–175\n\nRUN GUIDE\n\nHow it sho
 uld feel: Controlled and smooth\, like the middle of a good half. Not a ra
 ce\, and you should finish wanting more.\n\nHow to run it:\n• Jog 1–2 
 km and do 3–4 strides\n• 5 km at your Kerikeri goal pace: about 4:00
 –4:05/km for 1:25\, 3:53 for 1:22\, 4:10 for 1:28\n• Race shoes\n\nHea
 rt rate:\n• Expect about 165–175\, creeping up through the run\n• Ov
 er 178 before 4 km: you're not recovered enough for your target. Pick a sl
 ower one\n\nElevation: Flat. Under 20 m of climbing\, like the Good George
  course (+5 m) or your usual morning loop.\n\nBack off if:\n• If goal pa
 ce feels hard today\, ease off and pick a slower target on the Kerikeri ta
 b\n\nAfterwards: Carbs and protein within an hour. Rest tomorrow.\n\nhttps
 ://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
URL:https://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:akl-2026-11-19@joshuamorley.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261028T215149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261119
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261120
SUMMARY:Easy run + strides · 5 km
DESCRIPTION:• 5:30/km easy\, then 4 × 20 s relaxed strides.\n\nWhy: Keep
 s the legs loose before Saturday.\n\nFuel: None needed. Eat a normal carb-
 based meal afterwards.\n\nHeart rate guide (estimated from your recent run
 s\, may be off):\n• 5:25–5:35/km: easy\, HR 125–150. Over 150 means 
 slow down\n\nRUN GUIDE\n\nHow it should feel: Loose and easy\, with the st
 rides feeling quick and light.\n\nHow to run it:\n• 4.5 km at about 5:30
 /km\n• 4 × 20 s strides\, 60 s easy between\n\nElevation: Flat. Under 2
 0 m of climbing\, like the Good George course (+5 m) or your usual morning
  loop.\n\nBack off if:\n• Anything tight: skip the strides\n\nAfterwards
 : Pack for Kerikeri: race kit and shoes\, 2 gels\, breakfast\, and warm cl
 othes for the start.\n\nhttps://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
URL:https://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:akl-2026-11-21@joshuamorley.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261028T215149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261121
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261122
SUMMARY:Kerikeri Half Marathon · 21.1 km
DESCRIPTION:• Okaihau to Kerikeri\, point to point. Climbs about 95 m in 
 the first 7 km\, then drops about 275 m to the finish\n• Your target and
  km-by-km splits are on the Kerikeri tab\n• Last year: 1:37:54\n\nWhy: A
  fast course to finish the season on\, three weeks after Auckland.\n\nFuel
 : Breakfast 2.5–3 h before\, a gel 10–15 min before the start\, and on
 e more at about km 9\, after the top of the climb.\n\nHeart rate guide (es
 timated from your recent runs\, may be off):\n• 4:12–4:20/km: HR about
  158–170\n• 3:50–3:58/km: HR about 172–182\n\nRUN GUIDE\n\nHow it 
 should feel: The climb should feel controlled and almost too slow. The rac
 e is the descent and the last 5 km.\n\nHow to run it:\n• 10 min easy jog
  and 3–4 strides. A good warm-up matters more in a half than a marathon\
 n• Km 0–7.3\, the climb out of Okaihau: run by effort. Let the pace dr
 ift 10–20 s/km slower than goal\n• Km 7.3–9.6\, the steep drop: quic
 k\, light steps. Let gravity do it rather than braking or bounding\n• Km
  9.6–21.1: settle on your descent pace and race the last 5 km\n\nHeart r
 ate:\n• Climb: about 165–175. Over 178 before the top means you've gon
 e out too hard\n• Descent: about 170–180. HR rises less than the pace 
 does\, because downhill is cheaper\n• Last 3 km: anything goes\n\nElevat
 ion: About 95 m of climbing in the first 7.3 km\, then about 275 m of desc
 ent to the finish\, steepest from km 7.3 to 9.6. Numbers come from your 20
 25 GPS and may be a little off.\n\nBack off if:\n• Quads cramping or sor
 e on the descent: ease to a jog. It's three weeks after a marathon\, and t
 he result doesn't matter that much\n• Any sharp pain: stop\n\nAfterwards
 : Walk\, eat and drink. Then celebrate the season.\n\nhttps://joshuamorley
 .github.io/running-plan/#plan
URL:https://joshuamorley.github.io/running-plan/#plan
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
